title: Passwords are gone. It’s just you and your device.
type: update
date: 2026-06-03
createdAt: 2026-06-03T21:30:00Z
releaseAt: 2026-06-03T22:00:00Z
author: Richard D.
stream: strategy
tags: passkey, security, simplicity, ux

# The Change
body[1]{content}:
  "At Pharos, we want you to spend your time designing kitchens, not managing a list of passwords. That’s why we’ve made a big change: we’ve completely removed passwords from Pharos Kitchen Design. We’ve removed every line of password-related code from Project Prism. We aren't just 'preferring' passkeys; we’ve completely deleted the old way of doing things. No more AWS Cognito, no more 'forgot password' emails, and no more shared secrets. It’s just you and your device."

# What this means for you
details[3]{focus, description}:
  Nothing to remember, "You’ll never have to type a password here again. To log in, you just use what you already have—your phone’s FaceID, your laptop’s fingerprint reader, or a security key. It’s one touch and you’re in."
  Nothing to steal, "Since we don’t store passwords, there’s nothing for a hacker to take from us. Your login 'key' never leaves your device. We couldn't give it away even if we wanted to."
  Privacy by default, "We’ve stopped being the middleman for your identity. By using Passkeys, we’re making sure that you are the only one who can access your work."

# Why we did it
closing[1]{content}:
  "We realized that 'good enough' security wasn't good enough for the people building the world’s kitchens. We wanted a system that was actually secure, not just one that felt familiar. By deleting the password logic, we’ve made Pharos faster, safer, and more secure. It’s a simpler way to work. We think you’re going to love it."
